unning."]}, "overview": "Kansas's Senate race between incumbent Roger Marshall and Democrat Adam Hamilton has become one of the cycle's tightest. Polls since late September show the two essentially level, with results ranging from even to a narrow Hamilton lead, and headlines describe a contest Republicans did not expect to sweat. Marshall still benefits from incumbency in a state that backed Republicans by eight points at the presidential level, and prediction markets give him roughly a 60 to 65 percent chance of holding the seat.\n\nHamilton has out-raised Marshall roughly three to one, about 13.5 million to 4.8 million dollars, and holds a large cash advantage, while a favorable national environment and voter frustration with Washington keep Democrats competitive. Most race raters call the contest a tossup with a slight Republican tilt. The expected margin is about a point for Marshall, with wide uncertainty in a genuine tossup.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2893, "margin": -2.76}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3585, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4222, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4301, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3024, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2988, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4183, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4225, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.3457, "margin": -2.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.4294, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.3861, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.3969, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.4021, "margin": -1.8}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.3351, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-10-06", "p_dem": 0.3274, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-10-07", "p_dem": 0.4309, "margin": -0.5}, {"date": "2026-10-08", "p_dem": 0.3899, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-10-09", "p_dem": 0.3704, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "202